A profile keeps its extensions on a new Mac. Chrome checks protected preferences twice at load: first synchronously
with the legacy HMAC alone (an empty seed for this build, plus the machine's IOPlatformUUID), then, once the Safe
Storage key arrives, with the encrypted hash. The machine id changes when a profile moves to another Mac (Migration
Assistant, a Time Machine restore, a copied data directory), so every HMAC fails there and the first pass removed every
installed extension from extensions.settings, and reset developer mode, pinned tabs and startup pages, for good, before
the encrypted hash, which the migrated "Ardia Safe Storage" item still verifies, was read. In the field
(extensions_loaded, 14 days to 2026-11-26) every fresh drop checked was an install's first launch on different
hardware (M4 to M6, M4 Pro to M5 Pro, M1 to M5, M2 to M6, M3 Max to M5 Max), not an app update.

The first pass can't wait for the key: the startup profile loads synchronously (ProfileManager::CreateProfileHelper,
CreateMode::kSynchronous), PrefService::InitFromStorage CHECKs that a synchronous read finishes synchronously, and the
key arrives as a task posted to the same thread, or never while the Keychain is unreadable. So the first pass still
resets whatever fails its HMAC before anything is published, and what it resets that carries an encrypted hash is set
aside instead of lost, in the protected store's own file under ardia_awaiting_safe_storage (written by this filter,
never seen by the PrefService), off prefs.tracked_preferences_reset. A record moves through three states:
- pending: the value and its encrypted hash. The second pass decrypts the hash with the Safe Storage key and checks it
  against the value with Chrome's check. A key that is temporarily unavailable leaves it pending; a hash that doesn't
  verify drops it into prefs.tracked_preferences_reset.
- verified: it passed, and carries this machine's HMAC. The next launch's first pass puts it back, after that pass's
  own checks and before anything is published, only where nothing has taken its place, with its encrypted hash and
  an HMAC stamped through the transaction, so it loads like any installed extension. One whose HMAC no longer matches
  (the profile moved again before it came back) goes back to pending, for the key to check again.
- restored: the second pass of that launch checks the restored value against its encrypted hash again, strictly,
  with no HMAC fallback, and removes it and lists it if it doesn't verify. Then the record is gone.
Nothing comes back in the session it was reset in: an extension entry the loader never registered breaks shutdown
(PreferenceAPI clears content settings for every extension in the preferences). A pending or verified record is never
trusted on its own: the verified step is the HMAC Chrome's first pass already accepts for every protected value, and
the restored value is held to the Safe Storage key in its own launch. With kEncryptedPrefHashing off, or without
OSCrypt, there is no second pass and nothing is set aside. The reset list is now posted to the PrefService before the
second pass is, so a reset the second pass lists isn't overwritten by the first pass's list when the key is already
there.

